1
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:31,500
This is an advanced base somewhere in the far north,

2
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:37,200
one of the many Army and Air Force installations which have been constructed over much of the free world.

3
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:43,300
It is part of an integrated system of military bases set up a thousand miles from nowhere

4
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:45,260
to protect your freedom.

5
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:53,100
Whether manned by a handful of specialists or by thousands of military personnel,

6
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:57,939
there is one essential commodity which a base like this must have

7
00:00:57,939 --> 00:01:01,400
to carry out its mission, and that is power.

8
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:07,620
Obviously, operations and even human life in such areas depend on a reliable source

9
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,159
of electric power and heat.

10
00:01:10,739 --> 00:01:18,859
At present, a major portion of the supply tonnage to support an Arctic base consists

11
00:01:18,859 --> 00:01:24,759
of fuel oil required to generate this electricity and heat.

12
00:01:24,759 --> 00:01:31,500
Getting fuel to such bases is one of the greatest logistic problems faced by the military services.

13
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,519
Supply by ship is very seasonable, being limited to the short period each year when the waters

14
00:01:36,519 --> 00:01:40,719
in the area are ice free, usually less than 60 days.

15
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:48,539
Unfavorable weather conditions may make supplying such bases virtually impossible.

16
00:01:48,539 --> 00:01:52,219
Some of the more remote bases are supplied entirely by air.

17
00:01:52,219 --> 00:01:59,439
Needless to say, flying conditions in the Arctic are extremely hazardous and very unpredictable.

18
00:01:59,439 --> 00:02:04,840
Even in peacetime, these conditions add up to an extremely difficult logistical task,

19
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:11,219
in time of war, the problem is further complicated by the vulnerability to enemy attack of the long

20
00:02:11,219 --> 00:02:17,740
exposed supply lines. Since these bases are vital to our defense and must continue to operate at

21
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:24,800
all costs, something had to be done to try to alleviate this problem. Nuclear power offered

22
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:30,120
a promising solution to the logistic problem, since it was felt that nuclear power plants could

23
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:34,840
be built to operate over extended periods of time without refueling.

24
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:40,400
Thus, the great military advantage of such power plants would be the virtual elimination

25
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:48,140
of the effort required to transport and store bulky fuels for conventional plants.

26
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:53,719
For several years, the Army's Corps of Engineers and the Atomic Energy Commission in Washington

27
00:02:53,719 --> 00:03:00,020
had been conducting a number of studies exploring the use of nuclear power for military purposes.

28
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:12,860
The evaluation of all the data collected showed that the power requirements for certain Arctic bases could be met by nuclear energy sources with favorable cost comparisons and logistical savings.

29
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:26,000
Therefore, the AEC called upon its Oak Ridge National Laboratory to work with a core of engineers in developing a nuclear reactor design which might be applicable for military use.

30
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:38,439
After evaluating various reactor concepts, a group at Oak Ridge concluded that a heterogeneous pressurized water reactor offered the greatest promise of satisfactory performance.

31
00:03:39,259 --> 00:03:49,319
They then prepared a conceptual design, every component of which was to be air transportable, hence the terminology package power reactor.

32
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:55,439
In principle, a nuclear power plant is similar to a conventional power plant.

33
00:03:55,900 --> 00:04:01,479
The major difference is that the reactor replaces the furnace and fuel handling equipment

34
00:04:01,479 --> 00:04:06,979
with the advantage that the plant will operate for a long time before refueling is required.

35
00:04:07,799 --> 00:04:13,919
Basically, this is the way a pressurized water reactor, such as the one conceived at Oak Ridge, operates.

36
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:20,000
When these control rods are raised, a chain reaction takes place within this core.

37
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:26,319
Heat is generated by the chain reaction and heats the highly purified water in this primary loop.

38
00:04:26,379 --> 00:04:32,060
The water is maintained under pressure by the pressurizer to prevent boiling in the reactor.

39
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:38,639
It is this feature of the design which gives the reactor system its name, pressurized water reactor.

40
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,520
The pressurized water is circulated through the steam generator.

41
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:49,920
then back to the reactor by this pump. In the steam generator, heat is transferred

42
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:57,520
to the secondary water producing steam. The steam in turn runs this conventional

43
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:06,020
turbine generator producing electricity. The exhaust steam flows to the condenser

44
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:11,740
where it is condensed by cooling water pumped from a well or river. The

45
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:17,660
The condensate is then pumped through the feed water heater back to the steam generator

46
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:23,840
to be reheated and converted into steam, thus completing the secondary cycle.

47
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:28,960
All of the nuclear equipment in the primary system is enclosed in an airtight steel shell

48
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,139
called the vapor container.

49
00:05:31,139 --> 00:05:35,680
This container is a safety measure to prevent the possibility of release of radioactive

50
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:39,860
materials in the event of a major incident.

51
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:44,920
shielding from the effects of radiation is usually provided by surrounding the reactor

52
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:50,860
with a dense material, such as lead, iron, or concrete, with additional or secondary

53
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:55,199
shielding being provided by concrete surrounding the vapor container.

54
00:05:56,120 --> 00:06:02,160
An analysis of the Oak Ridge conceptual design conducted by the Operations Research Office

55
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:08,060
of Johns Hopkins University showed definite military and economic advantages were possible

56
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:11,040
at certain remote Arctic military installations.

57
00:06:12,319 --> 00:06:17,500
Based upon this analysis, the chief of engineers recommended to the Department of the Army

58
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:24,399
that a prototype nuclear power plant following the Oak Ridge design be constructed within the United States.

59
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:31,279
The Army's Corps of Engineers was given the development responsibilities for the Department of Defense.

60
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:37,860
The expense of the plant was to be shared jointly by the Atomic Energy Commission and the Department of Defense.

61
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:41,560
and construction was to accomplish three broad objectives.

62
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:47,500
The first was to solve the technical construction and operational problems

63
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:51,759
associated with a reliable nuclear power plant for military use.

64
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:56,920
The second objective was to provide firm cost information,

65
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:00,500
operating parameters, and engineering test data

66
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:03,759
necessary to adapt the system to a remote location.

67
00:07:03,759 --> 00:07:11,860
And the third objective was to provide a training facility for military specialists from all three armed services,

68
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:16,159
who would eventually be required to operate and service additional plants.

69
00:07:17,379 --> 00:07:21,759
While the AEC began an evaluation of qualified contractors,

70
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:27,399
personnel at Oak Ridge were engaged in analyzing certain aspects of the conceptual design.

71
00:07:28,219 --> 00:07:32,800
The feasibility of the core design was verified by an analysis on the computer.

72
00:07:33,759 --> 00:07:38,759
Investigations were made on the material to be used in the fuel elements.

73
00:07:38,759 --> 00:07:43,759
The effects of irradiation were studied by inserting a sample element into a test reactor

74
00:07:43,759 --> 00:07:47,759
and analyzing the irradiated samples in the hot laboratory.

75
00:07:47,759 --> 00:07:52,759
The tests indicated the materials were stable when subjected to radiation.

76
00:07:52,759 --> 00:07:56,759
Then the stability of the plant operation was checked.

77
00:07:56,759 --> 00:08:02,759
Here a technician is putting information into the analog to simulate the action of the control rods.

78
00:08:02,759 --> 00:08:13,759
And to complete the analysis, a series of critical experiments were made on a mock-up core to check the calculations and nuclear design.

79
00:08:13,759 --> 00:08:19,759
The results of these overall tests indicated that the design was basically sound.

80
00:08:19,759 --> 00:08:25,759
In the meantime, after a careful consideration of numerous locations throughout the country,

81
00:08:25,759 --> 00:08:30,819
The engineer research and development laboratories located within the U.S. Army Engineer Center

82
00:08:30,819 --> 00:08:35,879
at Fort Belvoir, Virginia, was selected as the tentative site of the prototype plant.

83
00:08:37,059 --> 00:08:41,899
Before a definite decision could be made, statistics had to be gathered on the conditions

84
00:08:41,899 --> 00:08:42,539
at the site.

85
00:08:42,539 --> 00:08:50,679
Data were gathered on such things as weather conditions, geological conditions, temperature

86
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:56,100
and chemical quality of the Potomac River, and radiological background of the area.

87
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:03,200
The United States Geological Survey, Coast and Geodetic Survey, and Weather Bureau contributed

88
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:04,460
notably to this effort.

89
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:10,640
From the information gathered, the Gunston Cove site was determined to be satisfactory

90
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:11,800
for construction.

91
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:18,000
In addition to offering an abundant source of cooling water, the location of the site

92
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:23,440
near the Army Engineer School at Fort Belvoir made the plant readily accessible for training purposes,

93
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:27,759
one of the three major objectives established for the project.

94
00:09:28,779 --> 00:09:35,299
Then on December 10, 1954, after consideration of 18 bidders from all over the United States,

95
00:09:35,879 --> 00:09:40,759
Alco Products, Incorporated, was awarded a contract by the Atomic Energy Commission

96
00:09:40,759 --> 00:09:44,179
to design and build the first package power reactor.

97
00:09:44,539 --> 00:09:52,399
An Army Corps of Engineers officer was appointed by the Atomic Energy Commission to supervise the construction and Alco retained

98
00:09:52,399 --> 00:09:58,220
Stone and Webster Engineering Corporation to design the non-nuclear portion and to construct

99
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:05,480
the entire plant. This was the first fixed fee contract with the guarantee of performance to

100
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:12,720
be awarded in the reactor field. The work was divided into five phases. Phase one covered the

101
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:18,420
preparation of the flow diagram and calculation of the heat balance for the primary and secondary

102
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:25,820
systems. During phase two, reactor scientists worked on preliminary designs and the data for

103
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:33,019
a hazards report. After successful presentations of this report to the AEC advisory committee on

104
00:10:33,019 --> 00:10:40,680
reactor safeguards, the detailed design began. During phase three, numerous engineers and

105
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:45,980
technicians made an analysis of the design, drew up specifications and

106
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:53,120
details of construction, and procured materials and equipment. During Phase 4,

107
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:59,980
components such as the reactor vessel were fabricated. And in Phase 5, field

108
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:05,440
engineers solved the many problems involved in actual construction. On

109
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:12,900
On October 5th, 1955, the clearing of the site got underway.

110
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:17,600
Sheep piling was used to hold back the earth during excavation to maintain the original

111
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:21,000
compaction of the soil for the building foundation.

112
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:26,940
Then, in accordance with the overall schedule, excavation was begun for the vapor container

113
00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:28,460
foundation.

114
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:33,320
This was followed closely by the laying of the foundations for the building.

115
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:37,920
Within 40 days, the concrete footings and foundation walls for the vapor container were

116
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:54,640
being poured.

117
00:11:54,639 --> 00:12:01,699
After this, the structural steel work was begun.

118
00:12:01,699 --> 00:12:11,139
Meanwhile, the vapor container foundation walls were completed, and after the forms

119
00:12:11,139 --> 00:12:18,080
were removed, the vapor container skirt was erected.

120
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:22,679
While some worked above, others worked below on the vapor container that would house all

121
00:12:22,679 --> 00:12:24,559
of the nuclear equipment.

122
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:31,780
The steel sections, which were fabricated by Bethlehem Steel Company, were carefully welded together to form an airtight shell.

123
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:36,660
The base of the vapor container was placed below ground to provide stability.

124
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:43,980
By late in March 1956, welders were working on the cylindrical portion of the shell.

125
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:52,140
During erection, all welded seams were tested thoroughly by radiographing to detect any cracks, however minute.

126
00:12:54,560 --> 00:12:58,600
And upon completion, the vapor container was again tested for leaks,

127
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:05,980
first by filling it with water under high pressure, and then after draining it by filling it with a helium air mixture

128
00:13:05,980 --> 00:13:10,000
and probing every inch of every seam for indications of leaking.

129
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:17,100
After successful completion of these tests, which indicated that the vapor container was airtight,

130
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:19,080
work was started on the interior.

131
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:23,980
The anchor bolts for the reactor vessel, steam generator, and pressurizer

132
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:25,019
were installed.

133
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:31,259
Then, the lower end of the vapor container was filled with concrete to provide a flat

134
00:13:31,259 --> 00:13:33,320
floor for mounting the nuclear equipment.

135
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:37,180
Rapidly, the building shell was nearing completion.

136
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:47,639
Concurrently with this came the installation of a thin steel inner lining in the vapor

137
00:13:47,639 --> 00:13:48,100
container.

138
00:13:48,899 --> 00:13:51,259
Section by section, the liner was put together.

139
00:13:52,060 --> 00:13:57,620
An inner layer of concrete, two feet thick, was raised by stages as the liner was erected.

140
00:13:57,720 --> 00:14:05,960
The liner acted as a form for this inner layer of concrete, as well as providing a smooth surface for possible decontamination.

141
00:14:06,879 --> 00:14:13,120
After the inner layer of concrete was added, the vapor container was surrounded by an additional three feet of concrete,

142
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:19,860
extending from ground level to the height of the building roof to provide additional shielding for operating personnel.

143
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:24,919
Condenser cooling water lines were laid from the Potomac River.

144
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:30,820
Elsewhere, Elko and its contractors were involved in other phases of the project.

145
00:14:30,899 --> 00:14:36,960
For example, in Coatesville, Pennsylvania, Lugan Steel Company was rolling the two-inch steel plates

146
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:41,320
to be used in the reactor vessel, the steam generator, and the pressurizer.

147
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:51,120
In Dunkirk, New York, ALCO personnel were fabricating the steam generator and other components.

148
00:14:52,120 --> 00:14:59,120
Meanwhile, officers and men from all three military services were being trained as operators and technicians

149
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:03,120
in anticipation of the day when the plant would begin operation.

150
00:15:03,120 --> 00:15:10,120
These personnel would also provide a cadre to train additional operators and technicians for future plants.

151
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:17,120
Twelve months after the first tree was felled, the structure was complete.

152
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:23,279
Now the equipment arriving as planned had to be installed, piece by piece, through the

153
00:15:23,279 --> 00:15:28,720
opening in the top of the vapor container.

154
00:15:28,720 --> 00:15:33,440
The steam generator was lowered through the opening and placed on the anchor bolts.

155
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:38,299
The shell of the steam generator was fabricated of two-inch thick steel plates and clad on

156
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:43,700
the inside with stainless steel to resist corrosion.

157
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:52,960
Next, the condenser was installed on the lower level of the building, and cooling water pumps

158
00:15:52,960 --> 00:15:55,720
were secured in place.

159
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:58,900
The turbine was positioned and lowered into place.

160
00:15:58,900 --> 00:16:04,340
The turbine is of conventional design and was manufactured by the General Electric Company.

161
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:09,740
It is this turbine that converts the heat energy of the steam into rotary motion.

162
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:14,220
This rotary motion is transferred through the reduction gears to the generator, which

163
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:18,960
converts it into electricity.

164
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:25,100
After the turbine was secured, the reduction gear box was installed and aligned.

165
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:30,120
Then the generator, also of conventional design and manufactured by General Electric, was

166
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:33,720
installed.

167
00:16:33,720 --> 00:16:40,720
Now the time had come to install the station service equipment and the electrical controls

168
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:47,200
in the central control room. While work was going on inside the building, provisions were

169
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:52,399
being made to provide parallel operation with the existing system of the Virginia Electric

170
00:16:52,399 --> 00:16:59,399
and Power Company. By July 30th, 1956, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, having completed

171
00:16:59,399 --> 00:17:04,339
laboratory, having completed the fabrication of the fuel elements, shipped them to Schenectady.

172
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:10,440
Here in Schenectady, Alco engineers experimentally determined the core characteristics in this

173
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:16,960
company-owned critical facility. The core was put through a series of tests and the control rods

174
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:24,079
were calibrated before being shipped to Fort Belvoir. At the construction site, the reactor

175
00:17:24,079 --> 00:17:29,319
vessel was being placed in the vapor container. This pressure vessel, which will contain the

176
00:17:29,319 --> 00:17:36,859
nuclear core is the heart of the nuclear power plant. Standing 13 feet 6 inches high and weighing

177
00:17:36,859 --> 00:17:43,700
32,000 pounds, the reactor vessel, like the steam generator, was fabricated of 2-inch steel plate

178
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:52,599
and clad on the inside with stainless steel. By November 1956, the installation of all the

179
00:17:52,599 --> 00:17:58,259
equipment in the primary system was completed. Each component was individually checked and the

180
00:17:58,259 --> 00:18:04,759
Integrity of the entire system was tested using radiographing, hydrostatic, and helium leak tests,

181
00:18:04,759 --> 00:18:07,480
similar to those carried out on the vapor container.

182
00:18:09,359 --> 00:18:15,279
In addition, every electrical instrument had to be checked to ensure its proper operation.

183
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:21,400
All of the equipment and instrumentation had been installed and tested

184
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:26,019
in the secondary or steam system, the turbine generator.

185
00:18:28,259 --> 00:18:31,480
The evaporator used to purify the water.

186
00:18:33,299 --> 00:18:34,779
And the condenser.

187
00:18:36,759 --> 00:18:50,079
And in the vapor container, all primary system components, including the primary shield tank in the background, which houses the reactor, along with the control rod driving mechanism in the pit.

188
00:18:50,859 --> 00:18:55,099
The insulated piping, which carries the highly purified, pressurized water.

189
00:18:55,100 --> 00:19:00,040
The primary coolant pumps which circulate the primary water

190
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:04,360
The steam generator located in the rear between the pumps

191
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:11,360
And the pressurizer which maintains the pressure in the primary system

192
00:19:12,280 --> 00:19:18,200
Non-nuclear tests had to be run on the entire circuit to demonstrate the integrity of the systems

193
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:25,279
upon completion of these tests construction was finished and the plant was ready to receive the

194
00:19:25,279 --> 00:19:34,380
nuclear fuel now the real advantage of nuclear power enough fuel for one and a half to two

195
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:40,160
years of operation of the plant was unloaded from a single plane onto one small truck

196
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:49,160
This fuel was stored in a small vault until it was inserted into the reactor.

197
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:55,480
The reactor is fueled with 45 of these elements, each made up of stainless steel clad plates

198
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:58,500
containing highly enriched uranium.

199
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:03,440
Seven of these elements have sections of neutron absorbing material joined to the top and are

200
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:07,680
used as control rods.

201
00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:14,120
To insert the fuel elements, specially designed handling tools are required.

202
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:22,640
The tool is inserted into the element and secured.

203
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:28,080
The element is then raised to the platform on top of the primary shield tank and lowered

204
00:20:28,079 --> 00:20:42,139
into the core.

205
00:20:42,139 --> 00:20:47,759
This core in the form of a cube measuring 22 inches on a side and weighing only several

206
00:20:47,759 --> 00:20:53,839
hundred pounds will fuel this nuclear power plant for one and a half to two years and

207
00:20:53,839 --> 00:21:00,000
will produce the same amount of electricity as approximately 60,000 barrels of diesel oil.

208
00:21:01,659 --> 00:21:08,279
With insertion of the fuel elements, the plant was complete and ready to operate. Zero power

209
00:21:08,279 --> 00:21:13,159
and low power tests were conducted, during which the proper operation of all equipment,

210
00:21:13,519 --> 00:21:18,599
both nuclear and conventional, was checked. The mechanical action of the control rods was

211
00:21:18,600 --> 00:21:28,560
carefully checked. At 1113 on April 15, 1957, the plant was put into operation. After a

212
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:36,140
short shakedown period, the 700-hour operational test was begun. The completed plant operates

213
00:21:36,140 --> 00:21:42,540
like this. When the control rods are raised, a chain reaction will take place inside the

214
00:21:42,539 --> 00:21:49,299
reactor, heating the water. These rods control the reaction rate by absorbing some of the neutrons

215
00:21:49,299 --> 00:21:55,859
produced during the fission process. When the rods are lowered into the core, the reaction rate is

216
00:21:55,859 --> 00:22:02,279
reduced by the absorption of neutrons by the poisons in the control rods. Therefore, when the

217
00:22:02,279 --> 00:22:08,279
rods are raised, the reaction rate is allowed to increase, and the highly purified water is heated

218
00:22:08,279 --> 00:22:16,039
and circulated under pressure through the tubes in the steam generator by the primary circulating pump.

219
00:22:16,660 --> 00:22:23,059
Pressure is maintained in the primary system by the pressurizer to prevent the water from boiling in the reactor.

220
00:22:23,960 --> 00:22:29,420
In the steam generator, heat is transferred to the secondary water surrounding the tubes,

221
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:33,139
converting the secondary water to steam.

222
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:40,140
The steam produced flows to the turbine generator, producing electricity.

223
00:22:42,300 --> 00:22:49,440
The exhaust steam leaving the turbine is then condensed in the condenser by cooling water pumped from the Potomac River.

224
00:22:51,040 --> 00:22:59,220
Once condensed, the secondary water is pumped through the feed water heater back to the steam generator, thus completing the cycle.

225
00:22:59,860 --> 00:23:05,480
The shielding of the reactor and its associated equipment is divided into two major sections.

226
00:23:06,220 --> 00:23:10,360
The primary shield surrounding the reactor vessel inside the vapor container,

227
00:23:10,860 --> 00:23:15,819
and the secondary shield consisting of the concrete inner liner of the vapor container

228
00:23:15,819 --> 00:23:18,539
and the three feet of concrete surrounding it.

229
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:28,299
At 3 p.m. on April 29, 1957, the plant was officially dedicated by Admiral Louis L. Strauss,

230
00:23:28,299 --> 00:23:34,619
Chairman of the Atomic Energy Commission, and Secretary of the Army Wilbur M. Brucker.

231
00:23:34,879 --> 00:23:39,039
In the name of the government of the United States and our great president,

232
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:48,779
dedicate this Army Package Power Reactor to the uses of humanity and for peace,

233
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:52,059
and may it unremittingly be ever thus.

234
00:23:52,059 --> 00:24:00,759
Ladies and gentlemen, with your permission now, I'll ask and invite Admiral Straw, my

235
00:24:00,759 --> 00:24:08,019
good counterpart, formerly of the United States Navy, to join with the Army, and not let my

236
00:24:08,019 --> 00:24:10,940
hand be the sole one that turns this switch.

237
00:24:10,940 --> 00:24:17,779
So, Admiral, if you'll come over when the moment arrives, we'll join together.

238
00:24:17,779 --> 00:24:35,139
This rotating radar antenna is symbolic of military uses of nuclear power, and conversely,

239
00:24:35,139 --> 00:24:39,819
the printing press is a symbol of civilian applications.

240
00:24:39,819 --> 00:24:46,240
Through the combined efforts of the AEC, the Army's Corps of Engineers, and civilian industry,

241
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:49,680
Atomic energy was being used to generate electric power.

242
00:24:49,920 --> 00:24:58,299
A package power reactor, every component of which could be transported by air and assembled at a remote base, was possible.

243
00:24:59,099 --> 00:25:04,160
Many logistical problems which had seemed impossible in the past now could be solved.

244
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:13,299
The Army Package Power Reactor is the forerunner of smaller, more mobile nuclear power plants to be developed for the armed forces.
